3 Usage
*******

‘pandoc-mode’ uses the transient package to create a keyboard-driven
menu interface to all options and settings.  The main menu is called by
pressing ‘C-c /’.  After that, everything is mostly self-explanatory.
From the main menu, you can run ‘pandoc’ on the buffer, view the output
buffer and the current settings, set the input and output formats, and
you can go to the options menu.

Note that if the menu bar is active, ‘pandoc-mode’ also provides a menu
in the menu bar.  It has mostly the same structure as the transient
menu, but it does not show the value of the options and it closes when
you change an option.  Below, I talk about the transient menu
specifically, but most of what is said applies to the menu bar menu as
well.

In the options menu, you can set options for running ‘pandoc’ on your
input file.  All Pandoc options can be set from the menu (except for a
few that do not make sense, e.g., ‘print-default-template’).  Note that
when you set options, they (by default) only apply to the current file,
and, more importantly, they only apply to the current output format.

This is actually an important point to remember: Pandoc makes it easy to
convert an input document to more than one output format, but the
settings may (and usually do) differ between output formats.  Therefore,
whenever you change and save a setting in ‘pandoc-mode’, it _only_
applies to the _current_ output format.  In practical terms, this means
that when you want to set options for a specific output format, you must
always set the output format first.

